<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heilongjiang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heilongjiang is the northernmost province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> along the Amur, whose Chinese name it bears. Its capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a>. In the eighth to tenth centuries its south-east lay within Balhae, whose Upper Capital stood near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ning-an">Ning'an</a>, and later it was the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> who founded the Jin dynasty. It became a province of China in 1907.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heilongjiang.jpg" alt="Heilongjiang" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 颜邯 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sifengshan_Reservoir_and_temple_of_Dragon_King,_Oct_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He moved the court from the original capital to the Central Capital and, around 756, to the Upper Capital, Sanggyeong, in the Mudan River valley near modern Ning'an in <b>Heilongjiang</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its success brought its composer office: Wang Shuangyin became a deputy to the National People's Congress, a member of the <b>Heilongjiang</b> provincial party committee, head of the province's model opera troupe and deputy director of its culture bureau, and was received by Jiang Qing.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li></ul></section>
